      "math_academy_id": "e-10584",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14162998/assets/bdaee2dee98e41505c8a35c617a8f4fc035125ea8d504efecbe58afc9f7fd06c.png)\n\nIn the figure above,  $m \\angle CBD = 5x - 5^\\circ$ and $m \\angle ABD =2x + 10^\\circ .$ If $\\angle CBD \\cong \\angle ABD$, find $m \\angle ABC.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $\\angle CBD \\cong \\angle ABD$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠CBD & =m∠ABD \\\\ 5x-{5}^{∘} & =2x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & =2x+{15}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={15}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle CBD$ is\n\n$$\nm \\angle CBD = 5x - 5^\\circ = 5 \\cdot 5^\\circ - 5^\\circ = 20^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, by the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ABC & =m∠CBD+m∠ABD \\\\  & =m∠CBD+m∠CBD \\\\  & =2\\cdot m∠CBD \\\\  & =2\\cdot {20}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
